(912309, 102350, 26321, '<td valign="top">oh wow i''m glad this one won cause it''s freaking ~*AMAZING*~</td>', 'oh wow i''m glad this one won cause it''s freaking ~*AMAZING*~', '2004-09-15 22:58:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(912316, 102666, 8367, '<td valign="top">The bird portion of this photo is excellent. I gave it a four, though, because of the background. To me, and as usual I''m in the minority, it''s obvious that you selected the bird and adjusted the background. The black edges are too sharp around the bird. If you put this up for a print, I''d suggest softening or blending the edges with the smudge tool or an equivalent technique.<br/>I don''t mean anything disrespectful to you. It''s a great photo. I''m just explaining my lower vote.</td>', 'The bird portion of this photo is excellent. I gave it a four, though, because of the background. To me, and as usual I''m in the minority, it''s obvious that you selected the bird and adjusted the background. The black edges are too sharp around the bird. If you put this up for a print, I''d suggest softening or blending the edges with the smudge tool or an equivalent technique.I don''t mean anything disrespectful to you. It''s a great photo. I''m just explaining my lower vote.', '2004-09-15 23:08:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(912334, 103102, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi Dwterry...<br/> <br/>This is a nice composition.  I like the ''steps'' that lead the my eye up to the statue at the top.  This is a really neat subject.  I''m curious if it is lit at night as well... there may be some strong opportunities for a night shot with this scene.  <br/> <br/>The post processing here created some damage in this photograph tho.  I don''t like the ''halo'' around the statue at the top of the frame.  I''m not sure if that is a result of the burn tool or not but it feels odd to me.  Looking at your camera settings, you may have been able to get the top area of this frame sharper as well with a smaller aperture.  I can''t really see any advantage of not having everything sharp in this scene...<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi Dwterry... This is a nice composition.  I like the ''steps'' that lead the my eye up to the statue at the top.  This is a really neat subject.  I''m curious if it is lit at night as well... there may be some strong opportunities for a night shot with this scene.   The post processing here created some damage in this photograph tho.  I don''t like the ''halo'' around the statue at the top of the frame.  I''m not sure if that is a result of the burn tool or not but it feels odd to me.  Looking at your camera settings, you may have been able to get the top area of this frame sharper as well with a smaller aperture.  I can''t really see any advantage of not having everything sharp in this scene... John Setzler', '2004-09-15 23:25:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(912347, 102666, 1031, '<td valign="top">That''s both a pretty bird and the name of my music publishing "company," named after the tract in Berkeley where I used to live before the place I''m in now.<br/> <br/>Also resembles the logo of a pro photo lab near where I work, <a href="//www.cantoo.com" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Cantoo.</a> <br/> <br/>Very nice job!</td>', 'That''s both a pretty bird and the name of my music publishing "company," named after the tract in Berkeley where I used to live before the place I''m in now. Also resembles the logo of a pro photo lab near where I work, Cantoo.  Very nice job!', '2004-09-15 23:32:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(912476, 102887, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Biggod53...<br/> <br/>This is a rather nice scene and location for a photograph :)  I like piers.  I visit them often when I travel to the North Carolina coast.  The fishermen and the pedestrians who are out for a simple stroll in the breeze are usually quite entertaining in their own rites.  If your coast is anything like mine, there is usually a constant breeze to some extent which keeps the aroma of the salt water in the forefront of my mind.  The water here looks relatively calm, so the breeze may not be as strong.  The North Carolina coast is not rough by any standards, but the average waves that constantly pound the shoreline are 3 to 5 feet in height in normal weather.  This looks like a relaxing place to be.  <br/> <br/>Nice work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Biggod53... This is a rather nice scene and location for a photograph :)  I like piers.  I visit them often when I travel to the North Carolina coast.  The fishermen and the pedestrians who are out for a simple stroll in the breeze are usually quite entertaining in their own rites.  If your coast is anything like mine, there is usually a constant breeze to some extent which keeps the aroma of the salt water in the forefront of my mind.  The water here looks relatively calm, so the breeze may not be as strong.  The North Carolina coast is not rough by any standards, but the average waves that constantly pound the shoreline are 3 to 5 feet in height in normal weather.  This looks like a relaxing place to be.   Nice work :) John Setzler', '2004-09-16 01:17:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(912673, 102676, 1368, '<td valign="top">til hamingju með 6 sætið og yfir 7 hefði verið flott með ribbon :D<br/>Ein flottasta mynd af skipinu sem ég hef séð :)</td>', 'til hamingju með 6 sætið og yfir 7 hefði verið flott með ribbon :DEin flottasta mynd af skipinu sem ég hef séð :)', '2004-09-16 05:37:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(913112, 102666, 8522, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Marjo:</b></div><hr/><i> The bird portion of this photo is excellent. I gave it a four, though, because of the background. To me, and as usual I''m in the minority, it''s obvious that you selected the bird and adjusted the background. The black edges are too sharp around the bird. If you put this up for a print, I''d suggest softening or blending the edges with the smudge tool or an equivalent technique.<br/>I don''t mean anything disrespectful to you. It''s a great photo. I''m just explaining my lower vote. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for explaining... I''m wondering if differences in monitor callibration have an effect here, if anything the selection seems a bit soft to me.</td>', 'Originally posted by Marjo: The bird portion of this photo is excellent. I gave it a four, though, because of the background. To me, and as usual I''m in the minority, it''s obvious that you selected the bird and adjusted the background. The black edges are too sharp around the bird. If you put this up for a print, I''d suggest softening or blending the edges with the smudge tool or an equivalent technique.I don''t mean anything disrespectful to you. It''s a great photo. I''m just explaining my lower vote.   Thanks for explaining... I''m wondering if differences in monitor callibration have an effect here, if anything the selection seems a bit soft to me.', '2004-09-16 13:52:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(913258, 103259, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Rooster...<br/> <br/>The composition on this photo doesn''t seem to really create any significant level of visual appeal.  The photo also seems to be a bit soft and lacks general sharpness.  I don''t know what this scene had to offer, but possibly a different camera angle/perspective would help eliminate some of the distractions from the statue itself.  A tighter composition that just highlights some interesting aspects of this statue could also be quite nice... something more than a simple documentary view.  Finding a composition within a composition is sometimes an excellent way to create some high impact photos :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Rooster... The composition on this photo doesn''t seem to really create any significant level of visual appeal.  The photo also seems to be a bit soft and lacks general sharpness.  I don''t know what this scene had to offer, but possibly a different camera angle/perspective would help eliminate some of the distractions from the statue itself.  A tighter composition that just highlights some interesting aspects of this statue could also be quite nice... something more than a simple documentary view.  Finding a composition within a composition is sometimes an excellent way to create some high impact photos :) John Setzler', '2004-09-16 15:42:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(913342, 103259, 7778, '<td valign="top">Just a lil observation: "Atlas" with the golden apples, ally of the Titans, condemed to carry the weight of the world vs. "(The) Atlas" - a book of world maps.<br/> <br/>I''d consider dropping the definite article.</td>', 'Just a lil observation: "Atlas" with the golden apples, ally of the Titans, condemed to carry the weight of the world vs. "(The) Atlas" - a book of world maps. I''d consider dropping the definite article.', '2004-09-16 17:01:43.000000', NULL, 0),
